  "summary": "The existing ideation has strong consensus on the three-tier extraction strategy and the test-first sequencing, but underexplores two practical dimensions: (1) the _check_for_changes() polling scalability problem called out in the original description, and (2) the concrete mechanics of characterization testing a Textual app. On the polling problem: _check_for_changes() (line ~2596) stats every feature file on each refresh cycle and compares snapshots — this is O(n) per tick and will degrade noticeably beyond ~100 features. The refactor should replace this with filesystem watch notifications (watchdog or inotify via asyncio) wrapped in a dedicated FileWatcher class that emits Textual Messages on change, eliminating polling entirely. This class naturally belongs outside tui.py regardless of the decomposition strategy, making it a good 'Tier 0' extraction that delivers immediate value and builds confidence in the refactoring process before touching any UI code. On characterization testing: Textual's built-in pilot testing framework (app.run_test()) is purpose-built for this — it can simulate key presses, button clicks, and screen assertions without a real terminal. The characterization suite should focus on three levels: (a) unit tests for the pure helper functions (lines 85-490) which require zero Textual infrastructure, (b) snapshot tests using pilot.app.query() to verify widget tree structure after key operations (board switch, feature selection, modal open/close), and (c) integration tests that drive multi-step flows (create feature → move to ideating → run pipeline → verify stage transition). The pure helper tests (level a) can be written immediately with no infrastructure investment and should gate the Tier 1 extraction. The pilot-based tests (levels b and c) require a mock Config and fixture board data but are straightforward with Textual's async test support. One underappreciated risk in the existing proposals: the SettingsPane and ScriptsPane classes (lines 1334-1557) post Messages that PipelineApp handles via on_settings_pane_settings_changed and on_button_pressed — extracting these panes to separate files is mechanically simple (they're already separate classes) but requires that the Message subclasses they define remain importable by both the pane module and tui.py. A shared tui_messages.py module for all cross-module Message types would prevent circular imports and should be created early in the extraction process."
